      "panel-about": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "",
          "id": 8,
          "links": [],
          "title": "About the data",
          "vizConfig": {
            "group": "text",
            "kind": "VizConfig",
            "spec": {
              "fieldConfig": {
                "defaults": {},
                "overrides": []
              },
              "options": {
                "code": {
                  "language": "plaintext",
                  "showLineNumbers": false,
                  "showMiniMap": false
                },
                "content": "**Where it comes from.** The City Council of Madrid's traffic cameras, as its traffic site [informo.madrid.es](https://informo.madrid.es) lists them in `CCTV.kml`: each camera's number, name, place and the address of the picture it last took (\"Tráfico. Cámaras\", [datos.madrid.es](https://datos.madrid.es/portal/site/egob), dataset 202088, CC BY 4.0, Ayuntamiento de Madrid). Pacha reads the list into a file once (`datasets/madrid-traffic-cameras/build.sh` in pacha-dashboards); the pictures are read by your browser straight from the city, which replaces them every few minutes.\n\n**How a view is worked out.** By the Kepler Plus map's Viewshed layer, in the browser: from each camera, lines of sight over a grid of cells about a metre or two across, 250 m in every direction, against the buildings of the base map (OpenStreetMap's, as OpenFreeMap serves them, with their heights) and the terrain (Mapterhorn). A cell is coloured when the street there, at ground level, can be seen. **10 m up** is the height the city's file gives every camera, a placeholder rather than each one's; **all round**, because the city does not say which way a camera points, and most of them turn. So a view is what a camera *could* see from there, not what it is showing now: the picture beside it says that.\n\n**What it leaves out.** Trees, kiosks, buses and the lamp posts in the way; buildings OpenStreetMap draws without a height count as about 8 m. The M-30's cameras stand over a road that is partly in tunnels the map cannot show, and the four in the Bailén tunnel have no view at all.",
                "mode": "markdown"
              }
            },
            "version": "13.3.0"
          }
        }
      },
      "panel-camera": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [
                {
                  "kind": "PanelQuery",
                  "spec": {
                    "hidden": false,
                    "query": {
                      "datasource": {
                        "name": "duckdb"
                      },
                      "group": "motherduck-duckdb-datasource",
                      "kind": "DataQuery",
                      "spec": {
                        "editorMode": "code",
                        "format": 1,
                        "rawQuery": true,
                        "rawSql": "-- The camera picked, on the map above or in Camera, with a ring 250 m round it: as far as its\n-- view is worked out. One row for both, so the map frames them together when another camera is\n-- picked. A camera in a tunnel has its picture but no view: it looks along the tunnel, not at the\n-- street over it.\nSELECT camera, camera_id, latitude, longitude, 'Up to 250 m' AS ring,\n       'POLYGON ((' || string_agg(round(longitude + 250 * sin(radians(a)) / (111320 * cos(radians(latitude))), 7)\n                                  || ' ' || round(latitude + 250 * cos(radians(a)) / 110574, 7), ', ' ORDER BY a) || '))' AS _geojson\nFROM read_parquet('http://feeds/datasets/madrid-traffic-cameras/cameras.parquet'), range(0, 361, 6) t(a)\nWHERE camera = $camera AND setting \u003c\u003e 'tunnel'\nGROUP BY ALL"
                      },
                      "version": "v0"
                    },
                    "refId": "A"
                  }
                }
              ],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "The camera picked, 10 m above the street, and what it has in sight within 250 m (orange, fading with distance); the street it cannot see is shaded. The ring is how far the view is worked out. Pick another camera on the map above or in Camera: this map goes to it.",
          "id": 3,
          "links": [],
          "title": "${camera}: what it sees",
